Basic Game Rules

Pyramid Solitaire is a very easy and fun variation of the lonely person's
favorite card game. First, after shuffling the deck, a "Pyramid" is built
by by dealing the cards off the top of the deck, and placing them face up
on the playing area. The first card goes at the top. The second and third
are placed on the lower half of the first card, with only the top half of
the first card still exposed. The second card covers the left lower corner
of the first card, and the third covers the right lower corner of the
first card. Next a third row is placed in a similar fashion, such that the
third row has three cards. This continues until the bottom row of cards
contain seven cards. Thus the entire Pyramid contains 28 cards, leaving
24 cards in the Deck.

The basic play operation is matching up two cards which are "free", such
that the value of the two cards add up to a sum of 13. Aces have a value
of one, Jack, Queen, and King have values 11, 12, and 13, respectively.
Therefore, Aces match up with Queens, 4's match up with 9's, etc. Kings
do not need to be matched with any other card, they may be picked up
singly. The two (or possibly one if a King) cards are then picked up
and placed in a discard pile.

What determines if a card is "free"? First of all, any card on the Pyramid
which does not have any cards on it is free. So, when the game first starts,
only the cards on the bottom row are free. Once some of them have been
matched and picked up, cards on the next level up become free.

Also, as with most variations of Solitaire, you may deal cards, three at a
time, from the Deck to a Pickup Pile (called just the Pile). The top card
on the Pile is also free and may be matched with any free card on the
Pyramid, if the matching criteria is met, ie, values add up to 13. As with
free Kings on the Pyramid, a King on the top of the Pile may be picked
up and discarded immediately. (And should be!!)

You may go through the deck as many times as you wish. If no matching plays
are made during a pass through the Deck, it is assumed that no matching plays
remain, and you lose the game. To win the game you must match and discard
all cards in the Pyramid. This obviously means that the last card in the
Pyramid (which was the first card turned over and placed on the Pyramid)
must be matched with the top card on the Pile, since it is the last card left
in the Pyramid (unless, of course, it is a King! <kindof like ending up
with an 8 while playing Crazy Eights!!>).

Got it?


Installation

The archive file contains the following files:

Solitaire            Game program
solitaire.doc        this file
solitaire.c          First part of the source
solitaire.h          Second part of the source
AIF.sol              MV AIF
icon.solitaire       MV Icon
release.notes        release notes

All of you OS9 wizards will readily know where each of these files
should reside. For non-OS9 wizards, suggested locations are:

/dd/games/AIF.sol
/dd/cmds/Solitaire
/dd/cmds/icons/icon.solitaire

I decided to include the source for Pyramid Solitaire with this release.
Please do not modify the game and upload your modifications to anyone
or anywhere. Please let me maintain control over this program. Feel
free to cut 'n' paste some sections of the code in some of your own
programs. I only included them to help give users some insight on
cgfx and MultiVue type coding.

NOTE: This game ONLY runs as a MultiVue application. It opens a type 6
graphics window, which uses 16000 bytes of memory. The program uses another
16K. Not sure where the other 8K comes from, but I found that a MFREE
command executed while the program is active shows 40K being used. So,
if you have a 128K CoCo 3, you must have at least 40K free to play this
game(doubtful!).

In the following instructions, "pointing" to an object means to move
the mouse pointer and "clicking" means to press the mouse button
(or if you are using a joystick, press the "Fire" button).

Since this game runs under OS9 and uses the "autofollow" mouse, the
keyboard mouse is also supported. If you don't have a mouse or joystick,
press CTRL/CLEAR to switch over to the keyboard mouse. For more
information on the use of the keyboard mouse, check page 2-2 of the
Multi-Vue manual.

To crank up the game, you must select the directory which contains the
AIF. When you do, the game icon will be displayed. Double click on the
game icon. The first screen is used to get your response as to what
type of monitor you are using, RGB or CMP. Just click on the word which
applies to your hardware.

An introductory screen then is displayed. When you click the mouse,
graphics images are produced and save away (done just once per
invocation), and the game then proceeds.

The Pyramid will then be set up for you. The Pyramid is in the center
of the screen, the Deck and Pile are on the left side of the screen and
three special commands are displayed on the right side of the screen.
These three special commands are more or less self-explanatory. Clicking
on them causes that function to be performed.

To select a card from the Pyramid, simply point to it and click the
mouse. To select the top card on the Pile, point to it and click the
mouse. Easy, huh? When you select a card either in the Pyramid or on
the top of the pile, it is highlighted by changing it's border from
black to red.

Once you have selected two cards, the program takes over. It the move
is valid, both cards are removed. If the move is invalid, ie, the values
of the two cards do not add up to 13, a message is displayed on the
error message line (line 24) and after a short pause, both cards are
de-selected.

If you select the wrong card, you may manually de-select it by clicking
in the word "CANCEL".

To turn over three more cards from the Deck to the Pile, point to the
red shaded Deck and click the mouse. What can be easier?!? If you click
on the Deck while you have a card selected, either on the Pile or on
the Pyramid, it will be de-selected before the Deck card is turned over.

At any time, you may give up on the current game and have a new game
started by clicking on "RE-DEAL". When you have finished playing and
want the program to exit, click on "QUIT".


I developed Pyramid Solitaire on a CoCo 3 with 512K, OS9 LII, OS9 C
Compiler, C Graphics Library, & Multi-Vue. It also depends on the
supplimental clib.l library written by Carl Kreider. My system also
has a Seagate ST225 20Mb hard disk (Burke & Burke CoCo-XTC Interface),
two 3.5" 720Kb floppies, and one 5.25" 360Kb floppy. I also have a
Tandy CM-8 RGB monitor, for which this game was specifically designed.
I did take a look at it on a TV and it didn't look too bad.


